The XORcyst is a set of tools for 6502 software development: the `xasm`
assembler and `xlnk` linker. This development tree is version 1.7.0-dev.
Run `xasm --version` or `xlnk --version` to identify an installed build.

Build and test
--------------

Normal builds use the checked-in parser, scanner, and configure script. A C
compiler, make, and a POSIX shell are required; Bison and Flex are not required.
On macOS, the command-line developer tools provide the CoreFoundation framework
used for filesystem path comparison. The test suite also requires Python 3.

    ./configure --disable-maintainer-mode
    make -j2
    sh tests/regression.sh

Use `make install` to install the tools. See INSTALL for prefix and compiler
options. CI runs the regression suite on Linux and macOS and runs symbol-table,
AST/JSON, and output-failure tests under ASan/UBSan on Linux. Tests use generated
fixtures and do not require game ROMs or a NESrev checkout.

See xorcyst.texinfo and the doc/ directory for the assembly language and linker
script reference. `xasm --help` lists all current command-line options.

Assembly and verification
-------------------------

    xasm game.asm -o game.o
    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg

Object output is intended for `xlnk`; pure-binary output requires all addresses
to be resolved by the assembler. To check a raw binary against a reference:

    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg --compare=reference.prg

For a reference containing a header or other regions, use `--compare-offset`
and `--compare-length`. `--compare-format=json` produces a structured report;
`--compare-max-mismatches` limits the displayed mismatches.
`--compare-cpu-base=ADDR` supplies a fallback CPU base for mismatches without
an address from the assembler's source map.

Listings and cross-references
-----------------------------

`--listing=FILE` supports `--listing-format=text|json|ndjson` (default text).
`--xref=FILE` supports `--xref-format=json|text|csv` (default JSON). CSV expands
the supplied prefix into `.symbols.csv` and `.refs.csv` destinations.

A complete JSON xref, including local/anonymous symbols, data references,
lexical owners, and structured instruction records:

    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg \
      --xref=game.xref.json --xref-include-locals=true \
      --xref-include-anon=true --xref-data=true \
      --xref-include-owner=true --xref-instructions=true \
      --listing=game.listing.json --listing-format=json

The optional instruction stream includes every emitted instruction, independent
of xref symbol filters. It preserves source spans, pre-fold expression trees,
and final instruction bytes. Lexical owners do not prove control flow.

To write instruction records separately:

    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg \
      --instruction-records-output=game.instructions.json \
      --dependency-manifest=game.dependencies.json

The standalone instruction output requires a dependency manifest in version 1.
A manifest records consumed-input hashes, lookup misses, invocation arguments,
and producer identity. It is published last, after input revalidation. Consumers
must still validate their own configuration and output identity when reusing
artifacts. See XASM_INSTRUCTION_RECORDS_SPEC.md,
XASM_DATA_DIRECTIVE_REFERENCES_SPEC.md, and XASM_DEPENDENCY_MANIFEST_SPEC.md.

FCEUX debugger names
-------------------

    xasm --pure-binary game.asm -o game.prg \
      --fceux-nl-rom-prefix=game.nes. \
      --fceux-nl-ram-output=game.nes.ram.nl

Use the reference ROM's pathname followed by a dot as the ROM prefix. Output
files are named `game.nes.0.nl`, `game.nes.1.nl`, and so on, using uppercase
hexadecimal physical 16 KiB PRG page numbers. Put these files beside that ROM.
The assembled binary must contain the complete raw PRG payload, without an iNES
header or CHR data. Unlabeled pages still receive empty files.

NL export includes local labels independently of xref flags. RAM names come
from dataseg labels and named direct memory operands below $8000; immediate-only
constants and anonymous operands are excluded. For a 16 KiB NROM program, add
`--fceux-nl-mirror-16k` to name both CPU windows. This option requires exactly
16384 emitted bytes. Higher-numbered files from earlier builds are not deleted;
the build process should manage obsolete sidecars. See XASM_FCEUX_NL_EXPORT_SPEC.md.

Analysis and diagnostics
------------------------

Optional analyses include `--xref-summary`, `--analyze-index-patterns`,
`--data-consumers`, and `--analyze-data-coverage`. Each supports text, JSON, and
NDJSON output. The corresponding destination flags are `--xref-summary-output`,
`--index-patterns-output`, `--data-consumers-output`, and `--data-coverage-output`.
Without a destination, these analyses write to stdout. When combining analyses,
give each one an explicit output path. See XASM_INDEX_BOUND_ANALYSIS_SPEC.md for
the evidence and limits of index-bound inference.

Unused EQU diagnostics are controlled by `--warn-unused-equ`,
`--Werror=unused-equ`, and `--Wno-unused-equ`. The raw-address audit uses
`--audit-raw-addresses`, `--audit-level=warn|error`,
`--audit-rom-range=LO-HI`, and `--audit-output-format=text|json`.

Output completion
-----------------

With NL export or a dependency manifest enabled, xasm rejects output paths that
refer to an input file or another output. Binary output and sidecars replace
their destination only after serialization, stream checks, and close succeed.
Existing files survive failed writes or renames, and owned temporary files are
removed. CSV finishes
both streams before publishing either file. Sidecar publication replaces a
symlink itself and rejects FIFOs, sockets, devices, and directories; use the
analysis stdout options for pipes. Sidecars preserve existing read, write, and
execute permission bits of regular files (including regular symlink targets).
New sidecars, and symlink replacements whose target is not a regular file or
whose permissions cannot be read, use mode 0666 filtered by the process umask.
NL export and dependency manifests also reject symlink paths that cannot be
resolved safely.

Publication happens one file at a time. A later failure can leave earlier
outputs updated. Stdout cannot be staged. Disregard
artifacts from an unsuccessful invocation; their existence does not establish
that the entire build completed. Diagnostic listings can be produced for failed
assemblies, and listing I/O failures are reported separately.

Exit statuses: 0 success; 1 assembly or binary-generation error; 2 CLI misuse;
3 destination validation or listing/xref/instruction-record/NL/manifest failure
(and comparison/audit I/O errors); 4 audit findings at error level; 5 comparison
mismatch; 6 xref-summary failure; 7 index-pattern failure; 8 data-consumer
failure; 9 data-coverage failure. An assembly error can take precedence over
a later diagnostic-listing error. Any nonzero status means the invocation failed.

Maintainer builds
-----------------

`version.h` is the version source for both tools and configure. After changing
it, regenerate configure with Autoconf 2.72; regenerate Makefile.in with
Automake 1.18.1 after Makefile.am changes. Keep those generated files in commits.
Use Bison 3.8.2 and Flex 2.6.4 for parser/lexer regeneration. Ordinary builds
should leave maintainer mode disabled.

Real-project compatibility checks are opt-in and keep project inputs read-only:

    python3 tests/verify_fceux_projects.py \
      --baseline /path/to/previous/xasm --candidate ./xasm \
      --source /path/to/project/asm/project.asm --report /tmp/xasm-check.json

The report compares binary bytes, diagnostics, and analysis outputs across
seven modes with NL disabled/enabled, and records paired timing samples.
